<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="50680" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring/CMN/538/50680.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2025" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025.xml">2025</calendarYear><term id="120251" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring.xml">Spring 2025</term><subject id="CMN" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring/CMN.xml">Communication</subject><course id="538" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring/CMN/538.xml">Seminar Rhetorical Theory</course></parents><sectionNumber>JDC</sectionNumber><sectionTitle>Intro Rhet &amp; Crit/Cult Studies</sectionTitle><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>This course will be an introduction to critical cultural approaches to rhetoric. We will review the origins, commitments and debates within critical/cultural rhetorical studies and study key concepts within this tradition, such as power, ideology, affect, and materialism. Students will develop a research project that contributes to one of the broad themes of the course (or that introduces one we ignore).</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>Open (Restricted)</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2025-01-21Z</startDate><endDate>2025-05-07Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="DIS">Discussion/Recitation</type><start>02:00 PM</start><end>04:50 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>W      </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>4007</roomNumber><buildingName>Lincoln Hall</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Cisneros" firstName="J">Cisneros, J</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>